  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "intervals-advanced",
      "title": "Intervals — Advanced",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** theory | **Tier:** advanced | **Difficulty:** 7/10\n**Prerequisites:** Intervals — Applied\n**Tags:** intervals, microtonal, extended"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Advanced interval study: enharmonic respelling, quarter-tone awareness (for world-music units), and analytical labels in scores."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "enharmonic-equivalence",
      "title": "Enharmonic equivalence",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "F♯ and G♭ are enharmonic in equal temperament — same pitch, different spelling depending on key context. Correct spelling follows diatonic scale membership."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "extended-and-altered-intervals",
      "title": "Extended and altered intervals",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Augmented and diminished intervals appear in chromatic harmony and jazz. Label the base size, then alter by semitone."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "world-music-note",
      "title": "World music note",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Many traditions use **neutral seconds and thirds** not captured by twelve-tone equal temperament. Regional lessons (Middle East, India) revisit intervals in those systems — Western labels remain your starting toolkit."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
